  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead they had from the start didn't survive the second inning.",
    "what_this_means": "Blade Tidwell lasted 4.1 innings and gave up six runs. That early exit forced the bullpen to work a chilly day game at Oracle in a game where runs mattered. And the bullpen answered with 3.5 innings of spotty coverage. Sam Hentges gave up two in the first inning he threw. JT Brubaker didn't record an out and let two more score. By the time the fourth inning ended, Colorado was up 13\u20137 and the game had already turned into a rout. Rafael Devers drove in three runs for the Giants' offense, which scraped together 11 hits but couldn't string them into anything that mattered late. Seven strikeouts in the lineup meant chances vanished clean.",
    "what_to_watch": "This team led after one inning and never led again. That kind of collapse. The early advantage erased in a single bad frame, then compounded by relief instability. Is the inconsistency that defined the whole season compressed into one game. The starter couldn't hold the fort. The bullpen couldn't shore it up. And the offense, even when it produced contact and moved runners, never converted urgency into runs when the margin was still there to close. Devers gave them moments. The structure didn't support a comeback from six down.",
